  "response": "교수님 때문에 오신 거라 여기 앉아 있는 것 자체가 좀 어색하고, 마땅히 해야 할 말을 찾기 어려우셨을 것 같아요. 그런데 방금 말씀하신 게 꽤 중요한 걸 짚고 있어요. 계획을 세우는 것까지는 된다는 거죠. 근데 실행하는 순간에 막히는 거고요. 그 막히는 순간에 보통 무슨 일이 일어나는지, 혹시 기억나는 순간이 있을까요?",
  "reasoning": "The client is a university student who was referred by a professor due to academic probation (two warnings, one more means expulsion). They're expressing a very common and painful pattern: they make plans, they genuinely intend to follow through, but they never do. There's a sense of hopelessness (\"this will be the same\"), but also a recognition that they came because they have to, and uncertainty about what to even talk about.\n\nKey things to notice:\n1. They feel compelled to be here (not self-referred) - there's some resistance or ambivalence\n2. They're not denying the problem - they acknowledge the pattern clearly\n3. There's a mix of resignation (\"it'll be the same\") and a small hope (they came)\n4. They don't know what to talk about - they need some direction or framing\n\nWhat would be helpful here:\n- Acknowledge the difficulty of being referred (not choosing to come)\n- Validate that the pattern they describe (planning but not following through) is something they're already aware of - they're not in denial\n- Not jump to solutions or ask \"why don't you follow through?\" which could feel blaming\n- Maybe gently reflect the tension between \"I know what to do\" and \"I can't do it\" - this is a key distinction. The problem isn't knowledge or planning, it's something else.\n- Offer a direction for the conversation that feels manageable - not overwhelming them with \"let's fix everything\"\n\nI want to avoid:\n- \"Let's figure out why you can't follow through\" - too directive, might feel like an accusation\n- \"That's a common problem\" - minimizing\n- \"What happens when you try to follow your plan?\" - could be useful but might feel like an interrogation right now\n- Being too warm/encouraging - they might feel patronized\n\nI think the right move is to reflect what they said - particularly the distinction between \"I can plan\" and \"I can't follow through\" - and gently name that this is actually an important starting point, because it narrows down what we're looking at. The problem isn't that they don't know what to do. Then maybe offer a small, manageable question or direction.\n\nLet me draft:\n\nSomething like: \"계획을 세우는 것까지는 된다는 거죠. 그런데 그 계획이 실제로 실행되는 순간에 뭔가 막히는 거고요.\" - This reflects and validates their self-awareness.\n\nThen maybe: \"그 막히는 순간에 보통 무슨 일이 일어나는지, 혹시 기억나는 게 있을까요?\" - This is a concrete, specific question that doesn't blame them and invites exploration of the actual mechanism rather than the moral failure.\n\nActually, let me also acknowledge the difficulty of being sent here. That's real and present.\n\nLet me refine:\n\n\"교수님 때문에 오신 거라, 여기 앉아 있는 것 자체가 좀 어색하고 마땅히 해야 할 말을 찾기 어려우셨을 것 같아요. 그런데 방금 말씀하신 게 사실 꽤 중요한 걸 짚고 있어요. 계획을 세우는 것까지는 된다는 거죠. 근데 실행하는 순간에 막히는 거고요. 그 막히는 순간에 보통 무슨 일이 일어나는지, 혹시 기억나는 순간이 있을까요?\"\n\nHmm, this is decent but let me check - am I asking too much? The \"기억나는 순간\" might be a bit much for someone who just walked in and doesn't know what to talk about. Maybe I should soften it or make it more open.\n\nActually, I think it's fine. It's specific enough to give them a concrete place to start, and it's not accusatory - it's asking about the experience, not the failure. It also implicitly validates that the problem is real and specific, not just \"you're lazy.\"\n\nLet me also consider: should I acknowledge the \"이번에도 똑같을 것 같고\" part? That's the hopelessness. Maybe I can touch on it briefly without being preachy.\n\nFinal version:\n\n\"교수님 때문에 오신 거라, 여기 앉아 있는 것 자체가 좀 어색하고 마땅히 해야 할 말을 찾기 어려우셨을 것 같아요. 그런데 방금 말씀하신 게 꽤 중요한 걸 짚고 있어요. 계획을 세우는 것까지는 된다는 거죠. 근데 실행하는 순간에 막히는 거고요. 그 막히는 순간에 보통 무슨 일이 일어나는지, 혹시 기억나는 순간이 있을까요?\"\n\nI think this works. It's warm without being saccharine, validating without minimizing, and gives them a concrete direction.\n",
